  "independent_visible_observations": {
    "comparison_limit": "These are independent visible comparisons, not proposed frozen criteria or a grade. No target pixel measurement was converted into camera, envelope, rig or functional requirements.",
    "fixed_target": [
      "Retains a single generic blue fish with paired eyes, dorsal fin, side fins and broad tail; its body appears fuller, with a more rounded head/muzzle and softer fin/tail edges.",
      "Eyes are much more expressive relative to the head, with large pale whites, blue iris transitions and bright glints. A turquoise muzzle surrounds a readable oval dark mouth.",
      "The caudal tail has a curved two-lobed top edge and curved internal blue/cyan regions. Side fins read as fuller shaped fins rather than pointed triangles.",
      "The body uses large curved/scalloped blue regions; a central raised dorsal element tapers along the back. Cyan cheeks/belly accents and broad highlights describe volume.",
      "Some target highlights are distinctly shiny; actual GLOBAL style supplies a softer broad-lit reference. Neither image sets a future camera, world envelope or pixel registration."
    ],
    "global_style": [
      "The actual GLOBAL image is a green angular leafy plant in an orange/gold beveled pot. It preserves deliberate faceted planes while making them feel full through broad light gradients, warm highlights and soft internal shadow transitions.",
      "Pot rim and corners have visible thickness and rounded bevel response. Highlights remain broad and material forms remain readable; the reference does not require replacing the fish blue identity with plant/pot colors."
    ],
    "original": [
      "A compact blue fish faces toward the viewer with its caudal tail at the far end, a tall central dorsal ribbon, two pointed side fins, paired white eyes with dark pupils and tiny white glints, and a small dark mouth.",
      "Body and tail show distinct flat triangular/polygonal planes. The body is comparatively narrow; the fins and tail are angular and read as thin wedges. Eye whites are small round forms, and mouth detail is subdued.",
      "Three blue-value regions and pale eye whites establish a clear generic blue-fish identity. Most of the canvas is transparent; this is an observation about the supplied snapshot, not a registration or size requirement."
    ]
  },
